WEBVTT

cue-VID_U1_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
An entrepreneur organises resources, takes risks and makes decisions in the hope of profit. Soffia Khan founded Northbeam Couriers with three vans. Successful entrepreneurs show resilience when deliveries fail, creativity in new routes, confidence, determination and the ability to communicate with drivers. Because she combined land, labour and capital, she created a business. Therefore enterprise is the organising, risk-taking factor of production.

cue-VID_U1_03-2
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Soffia wrote a business plan before launching. It contains an overview or summary, clear objectives, the resources needed, market research, a marketing strategy, operations, finance forecasts and the people required. A plan helps secure finance. It forces the owner to test assumptions before launch. Because the plan reveals weak demand early, therefore the business can change course. So a written plan reduces risk and guides decisions.

cue-VID_U1_03-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Business size can be measured by the number of people employed, the value or volume of output and sales, or capital employed. Profit is not a method of measuring business size. Northbeam Couriers can be large by staff count but small by sales value, while a specialist firm may show the reverse. Because no single measure is perfect, therefore exam answers should compare at least two.

cue-VID_U1_03-4
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Let's measure Northbeam Couriers. At launch it employed forty people and generated one point two million dollars of sales revenue. After expansion it employed one hundred twenty people and reached three point eight million dollars of sales. Capital employed rose from four hundred thousand to one point one million dollars. The employee counter shows growth; the revenue bar shows a bigger jump. Value added rose too.

cue-VID_U1_03-5
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Growth can be internal, also called organic growth. Northbeam Couriers develops new products, such as refrigerated delivery, and new markets, such as a neighbouring city. Because the firm uses its own resources and existing staff, therefore it grows gradually and keeps control. So risk stays lower than a takeover, although expansion is slower. The added value earned from each delivery provides funds for many more vans and trained drivers.

cue-VID_U1_03-6
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:40.000
External growth happens through a merger or takeover. Northbeam Couriers merges with Pinnacle Parcel Works, another courier firm at the same stage of production. That is horizontal integration. The two company blocks slide together and form one larger block. Alternatively, a takeover occurs when one business buys another. Because the combined firm serves more customers and routes, therefore it gains market share and bargaining power.

cue-VID_U1_03-7
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Vertical integration joins firms at different stages of the same industry. Northbeam Couriers buys Vertex Van Bodies, a supplier, for backward integration. Later it links with Doorstep Direct, a delivery network closer to customers, for forward integration. On one side, this gives control of supply and access to customers. On the other side, the firm may lack manufacturing skill, and growth can bring poor communication and loss of control. Therefore managers must weigh control against complexity.

cue-VID_U1_03-8
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Final check. Northbeam Couriers buys Vertex Van Bodies, which makes the vans it uses. Is that horizontal or vertical integration, and which direction? Buying a supplier is backward vertical integration, because the supplier is at an earlier stage of production. Horizontal integration would mean buying another courier firm. Name two valid measures of business size, and explain why profit is not one. Watch the blocks move into place as you answer.

